      <p class="article__eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1 class="article__title">Understanding the Bible on the Bible&rsquo;s terms</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>Most of us were handed the Bible along with a set of instructions for how
      to read it &mdash; instructions that came from somewhere else. A tradition, a
      teacher, a method, a favorite author. Sometimes those instructions help.
      Often they quietly get in the way, until we&rsquo;re reading the Bible on
      <em>our</em> terms, or someone else&rsquo;s, instead of its own.</p>

      <p>This site exists because I&rsquo;ve become convinced of something simple and,
      I think, freeing: <strong>Scripture comes with its own way of being read.</strong>
      The Bible is not a pile of raw material waiting for us to supply the method.
      It shows us, on its own pages, how it is meant to be understood &mdash; how its
      own writers read what came before them, how meaning runs from the Author
      through the text to the reader, and how a word written long ago actually
      reaches the person holding it today. Learning to read it that way is not a
      technique reserved for scholars. It is available to anyone willing to let the
      text lead.</p>

      <p>That conviction is what the name points at. A one-way sign doesn&rsquo;t give
      you options; it tells you which way the road goes. Meaning in Scripture runs
      one direction &mdash; from God, who spoke, to us, who receive. We don&rsquo;t
      build our way up to the text or bend it back toward ourselves. We follow where
      it already points.</p>

      <h2>Who&rsquo;s behind it</h2>

      <p>I&rsquo;m Jason Kruis. I&rsquo;m a pastor, and I&rsquo;ve spent years working
      in the area of biblical interpretation &mdash; how we get from the words on the
      page to a right understanding, and from a right understanding to a life lived
      before God. Much of what you&rsquo;ll find here began in the ordinary work of
      preaching and teaching and sitting with people over open Bibles, and only
      later got worked out more carefully. I care most about the people this is
      finally <em>for</em>: the believer in the pew, the pastor in the study on
      Saturday night, my own children &mdash; anyone who wants to read the Bible with
      confidence that they&rsquo;re hearing what God actually said, and not just what
      they brought to it.</p>

      <p>I&rsquo;m not selling anything here. Everything on this site is offered freely,
      and shared under a license that lets you copy and pass it along. If it helps
      you read the Bible more faithfully, it has done its job.</p>

      <h2>What you&rsquo;ll find</h2>

      <p>The library is organized by topic. Under each one you&rsquo;ll find writing
      &mdash; and, over time, talks, teaching, and other resources &mdash; aimed at
      helping you read a given part of Scripture, or a given question, the way it was
      written to be read. Some pieces are short and practical. Some go deeper. All of
      them are trying to do the same thing: get out of the way, and let the text point.</p>

      <hr>

      <p class="ref">This page is a first draft &mdash; a starting point to revise
      into your own words.</p>
